/**

\page InteractionHowTo Interaction Related Examples in MITK

\tableofcontents

\section EnableDisableInteraction How to keep Display Interaction from reacting to the left mouse button

Display interaction (setting the crosshair, panning, zooming, scrolling, ...) is implemented by
mitk::DisplayActionEventBroadcast, one per multi widget, each registered as an mitk::InteractionEventObserver.
Observers are notified about every event that no mitk::DataInteractor handled. A tool that is an observer
itself, like the segmentation tools, therefore gets the same left mouse button press as the display
interaction, and a single click would both draw and move the crosshair.

Such a tool blocks left mouse button presses for display actions while it is active:

\code
#include <mitkDisplayActionEventBroadcast.h>

// Member
mitk::DisplayActionEventBroadcast::LeftButtonBlock m_DisplayLeftButtonBlock;

// On activation
m_DisplayLeftButtonBlock = mitk::DisplayActionEventBroadcast::BlockLeftButton();

// On deactivation (destroying the block releases it as well)
m_DisplayLeftButtonBlock.Reset();
\endcode

The block applies to all broadcasts, including broadcasts created while it is held, and switching the
interaction scheme does not lift it. Blocks are counted, so independent tools do not need to coordinate
their blocks. All other display interaction, like the mouse wheel, the middle and right mouse buttons,
and the keyboard, stays available.

Tools derived from mitk::Tool do not hold a block themselves but switch on BlocksDisplayLeftButton in
their constructor; mitk::SegTool2D does this for all 2D segmentation tools.

A mitk::DataInteractor does not need a block for events it handles, since handled events are not passed
on to the display interaction.

\section ExclusiveInteractionHowTo How to keep tools of different views from being armed at the same time

A tool is armed while it waits for or processes input in the render windows, for example an active
segmentation tool, a measurement figure that is about to be placed, or a point list in adding mode. Tools of
different views know nothing about each other, so without coordination a user could arm a tool in one view
while a tool of another view is still armed, and both would react to the same clicks.

mitk::ExclusiveInteraction lets at most one tool be armed at a time. A component acquires a claim when it arms
a tool and passes a revoke function that disarms it again. Acquiring calls the revoke function of the current
claim first:

\code
#include <mitkExclusiveInteraction.h>

// Member
mitk::ExclusiveInteraction::Claim m_ExclusiveInteractionClaim;

// When the user arms the tool
if (!m_ExclusiveInteractionClaim.IsActive())
{
  m_ExclusiveInteractionClaim = mitk::ExclusiveInteraction::Acquire([this]() {
    m_ToolButton->setChecked(false); // Disarms the tool through the button's toggled() handler
    return true;
  });

  // The armed tool of another view refused to be disarmed.
  if (!m_ExclusiveInteractionClaim.IsActive())
  {
    const QSignalBlocker blocker(m_ToolButton);
    m_ToolButton->setChecked(false);
    return;
  }
}

// When the tool is disarmed (destroying the claim releases it as well)
m_ExclusiveInteractionClaim.Reset();
\endcode

Keep the claim while switching between tools of the same component: acquiring again would revoke the own claim
first. The revoke function may return false to keep the claim, for example after the user declined to discard
unconfirmed results. The previous tool is disarmed before Acquire() returns, so the new tool never has to share
the render windows with it.

Segmentation tools do not use claims directly. Tools that take input in the render windows switch on
ClaimsExclusiveInteraction in their constructor, and mitk::ToolManager holds the claim while such a tool is
active. Before another view disarms a tool with unconfirmed results, the tool manager asks the function set by
mitk::ToolManager::SetDeactivationConfirmation(), which the tool selection box provides.

Display interaction and the interactors of data that can merely be edited, like the control points of finished
planar figures, do not take part. They stay available next to the armed tool.

*/
